Warp divergence occurs when threads within the same warp take different execution paths due to conditional logic (e.g., `if/else`). The hardware serializes these paths: it executes one path for the active threads, then the other path for its active threads, effectively disabling inactive threads. This significantly reduces parallelism and wastes execution cycles, as the total execution time becomes the sum of the times for all divergent paths.
```c++
// Inside a kernel
if (threadIdx.x < 16) { // Threads 0-15 take this path
    // Do something
} else { // Threads 16-31 take this path
    // Do something else
}
```
This code causes divergence within a 32-thread warp.
